{"title":"Cauchy's Criterion","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Cauchy's Criterion</b> is a result in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Analysis\" title=\"Analysis\">analysis</a> that states that a sequence of real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb07e848f9188a9fd706d99ab6513a7d949c48a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> converges if and only if it is a Cauchy sequence.  A sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb07e848f9188a9fd706d99ab6513a7d949c48a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> is considered Cauchy if, for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/4/ca47de052eb3fb5454694d9bce3561ebff831a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />, there exists an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/9/5d99acd4014ffa71cf69587a648b78fdbff1d7f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> such that for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/3/a23edda0a68213f587da9bfc6bd3852f62da54bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n\\ge N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/4/dd41887c34bff3ad464b8051f7cb3ec72212a90c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n-a_m|&lt;\\epsilon$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"107\" height=\"20\" />.  The criterion is named after Augustine Louis Cauchy, a prominent mathematician known for his results in algebra and analysis.  \n</p><p><i>Proof</i>: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/b/dcb62e1c33ff6d5519e02f8997abe1408dffed54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)\\to a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/4/ca47de052eb3fb5454694d9bce3561ebff831a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> be arbitrary.  Choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/9/5d99acd4014ffa71cf69587a648b78fdbff1d7f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> such that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/845c0da210ec3bb12d45628b74e37402799d9695.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"17\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/b/86ba66f699c201d1c1deddcc5c0225e16c5fa01e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n-a|&lt;\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" />.  By the triangle inequality we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/d/64dc94cbf3a7ea39862d6d75745e99241d58c91b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|a_n-a_m|\\le |a_n-a|+|a-a_m|&lt; \\frac{\\epsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}=\\epsilon\\]\" width=\"346\" height=\"32\" />\nwhich proves that this sequence is indeed Cauchy.  Now for the reverse direction.  We first show that if a sequence is Cauchy it is bounded.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/9/4b989b85ee684ffa8a14baaced50b4c00b72b80f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c7049df3dae75009d7169ebb05474c3c97334cd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n-a_m|&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/9/d296aeb7efc7c2cd4852b06be5eb3806e4917699.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n,m\\ge N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />.  Then we see that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/6/f867566e669b660d22dec7de552706e4e58b2535.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/8/3f8fec14005b805caebe3e809a4ac5c861fc8960.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n|&lt;|a_N|+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" /> which means it is bounded by \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45dd7b389fa0846642b2303b54c6d06a877ae08.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M=\\max\\{|x_1|,|x_2|,\\ldots,|x_{n-1}|,|x_N|+1\\}\\]\" width=\"317\" height=\"18\" /></center>\n<p>so it is therefore bounded.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f07944af329150363407b27848ce2fefd3d1f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n|\\le M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" />, by the Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb07e848f9188a9fd706d99ab6513a7d949c48a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> has a convergent subsequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/0/3e0edfc450d45eb595196d399e963837179de612.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{n_k})\\to a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" />.  We show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/b/dcb62e1c33ff6d5519e02f8997abe1408dffed54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)\\to a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"20\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb07e848f9188a9fd706d99ab6513a7d949c48a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> is assumed to be Cauchy, set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/9/5096bc343b7a27055b04ead0ab2df4f905c8336d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n-a_m|&lt;\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"34\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/3/a23edda0a68213f587da9bfc6bd3852f62da54bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n\\ge N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />.  Additionally, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/0/3e0edfc450d45eb595196d399e963837179de612.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{n_k})\\to a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/4/5047f0bf296a06f1332367393dd32339484c03ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_{n_k}-a|&lt;\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"34\" /> and we see that by the triangle inequality \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/7/bc714fb1187a6f25fcb9cfd4bd75c6fee5717fae.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|a_n-a|\\le |a_n-a_{n_k}|+|a_{n_k}-a|&lt;\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\epsilon}{2}=\\epsilon\\]\" width=\"353\" height=\"32\" /></center>\n<p>which proves that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb07e848f9188a9fd706d99ab6513a7d949c48a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> is convergent.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018062221\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.010 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.018 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 88/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5417/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:22781:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018062221 and revision id 174736. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}